the main problem that we face is that  our current growth is not at a recovery  speed so what a recovery means is that  you grow at more than the rate that you  averaged over the past in order to  recover you got to get back to where you  were and so if you had a shock like a  big recession that we just encountered  we have to grow at more than the  long-term rate so the long-term rate in  the United States is about 3% we usually  grow at about 3% per year unfortunately  since the recovery we've been growing  not at a rate above 3% which is what we  need to get back but actually at a rate  below 3% so rather than recovering we're  actually getting further and further  away from where we need to be  so we need to get on a better trajectory  and the best way to do that is to  increase investment to increase  productivity and I view that as coming  primarily from four policies one is a  lower and better more efficient tax code  two would be a better regulatory  structure so a more cautious eye to  regulation and regulation that enhances  economic growth the third would be  getting the budget under control so not  growing the debt at record paces and the  fourth would be engaging in more  international trade if you look at the  US population we're less than five  percent of the world right now we're a  much bigger part of the world economy  but in order to continue that that  status in the world in terms of economic  structure we've got to be using the rest  of the world as our market and not just  the United States and that means more  trade we've been way too reluctant to  extend our trade over the past three to  four years and we need to do that more  much more aggressively most economists  believe that we should move to something  like a consumption tax what is a  consumption tax it means it only taxes  consumption it does not tax income there  are a variety of technical reasons for  that but this is pretty much  uncontroversial in the sense that almost  all economists think this is the best  way to go the problem is making it  progressive so the easiest way to do  that would be to keep something like the  current progressive tax structure but to  allow for no taxation of investment if  you do that you're basically saying we  tax only consumption a variety of ways  to do that again this is technical stuff  but the simplest way to do that is to  allow businesses to expense that means  deduct from their taxes all investment  that they make  that's a pro-growth strategy and it's  one that most economists tend to like  even if you tax the rich as much as you  can possibly tax the rich again using  the president's numbers there's just no  way we're going to even come close to  closing the gap between his projected  spending and his projected tax revenue  by taxing the rich that's already built  into his numbers and we can't get there  so what that implies is that if we want  to continue on this path of spending we  will have to raise the average tax for  the average American by 50%  that means everybody would be paying  about 50% more in taxes the most likely  way that that would come about would be  to impose a v80 tax which is like a  federal retail sales tax on the economy  that would have negative impacts on  economic growth and so you know that  would basically make us look a lot more  like Europe we have very large projected  increases in spending most of this comes  from Medicare and Medicaid Social  Security is also a problem but Social  Security is a problem that most people  think we can deal with more readily  there are some pretty clean solutions to  that problem Medicare and Medicaid are  much more problematic as we look down  the road and what we need to do is not  cut Medicare and Medicaid but we need to  figure out ways to limit the growth in  those expenditures to move to a more  efficient healthcare system that  probably means more incentives on the  margin for people to bear their own  costs of their own health care make  decisions in a more rational way some of  the Obamacare measures are tries our  attempts to move in that direction we'll  see if they work many of the Obamacare  measures are subsidies to the middle  class that actually work in the wrong  direction so net you know we'll have to  see if that's cost saving the president  claims they are I think many are  skeptical that they will be cost saving  in the long run I think most people who  have analyzed that including the  Congressional Budget Office have altered  their opinions and revised upward the  cost associated with president's health  care bill but we'll see  I think that the Republican view that  you should not raise taxes and that you  have to focus on the spending side comes  from the view that incentives matter and  incentives matter a whole lot and if you  start taxing people eventually the money  is going to move abroad and by the way  that's not because Americans are going  to invest elsewhere the marginal dollar  of investment in the United States is  not coming from an American it's coming  from a Brit or from a Dutch person or  from a German who's investing in the  United States and they can go anywhere  with their money so as we start raising  taxes those money those dollars are  going to go elsewhere and that's the  concern I think the Democrats have a  somewhat different view their view is  that we can do a good bit of  redistribution help the poor to a  significant extent without too much  effect on incentives without too much  effect on growth  during their session productivity  actually went up and you'd say I could  productivity go up during a recession  that's weird well the reason for that is  that what happened was although output  fell demand for products fell employment  fell by even more so what that means is  that the remaining workers actually had  to work harder they had to make up for  their colleagues who were laid off or  the people who were not rehired during  the recession and in fact that happened  and Catherine Shaw one of my colleagues  at the Business School and one of our  former students who's now at the  University of Utah Chris Naunton  and I have been doing a study looking at  that specifically in the context of a  firm where we can actually measure  productivity and we do see people  working harder during the recession  perhaps as a result of their being  somewhat more afraid that they'll lose  their jobs and they want to protect  their jobs and are willing to work  harder to do it the question is whether  that kind of efficiency enhancement that  occurs in a short period of time can  persist for a long time and whether that  can continue to grow and I'm doubtful so  I think these increases in productivity  that we saw for the short run are not  sustainable over the long haul in order  to get productivity to grow over the  long haul we've got to do a number of  things most of it comes from the  investment side but it means investment  in physical capital investment in human  capital that's what Stanford does we are  producers of human capital and extremely  good producers of human capital and  technology which is derived from those  two so I think that's the that's the  formula for the long run it's not making  people work harder
 

